beren_belagund@hotmail.com (Greg Welty) writes:
> Hi folks,
> 
> I just wanted to make sure of something. If I'm a barbarian doing
> #twoweapon combat, basically it doesn't matter *at all* to my 'to hit'
> bonuses what my skill levels are in my two weapons. The only thing
> that matters is my skill in #twoweapon. Is that correct?

No. It won't matter if you get the individual weapon skills to skilled
or expert, since your max for two-weapon is basic. But unskilled weapons
will still perform worse than basic ones.

> I base this on the following paragraph in
> <http://www.spod-central.org/~psmith/nh/weap-343.txt>:
>
> # +   A penalty if using two weapons and/or riding while poorly skilled;
> #     see 2-WEAP +HIT and RIDING +HIT in the weapon skills table above.
> #     (For the former purpose, your skill is considered to be the lower
> #     of your actual two-weapon skill and your skill in your primary
> #     weapon.)
> 
> So if the best I can do in #twoweapon is 'basic', that translates to
> -7 to hit and -1 damage. But the worst I can do in an individual skill
> is 'unskilled', which translates to -4 to hit and -2 damage. Since the
> *lower* of these is -7, is basically doesn't matter what my skills are
> in my individual weapons, if I'm #twoweaponing.

The WEAPON +HIT table is ignored when two-weaponing, so the -4/-2 is
irrelevant. It's the lower of the _skills_ that's under discussion,
not the lower of any modifier that might be applied; whichever skill
it is, it's looked up on the 2-WEAP table.

> I can get those skills
> as high as I want, and I'll still be subject to the #twoweapon
> penalties, and no individual skill bonuses.

"as high as you want", yes. But not as low as you want: if you have
basic skill in two-weapon and are unskilled in your primary weapon,
the penalty will be -9. If you are basic in both, the penalty will be
-7.

> Ditto for damage bonuses:

Same analysis, except that it's applied to each weapon individually.

> Well, OK, I might lose a point of damage if I #twoweapon an unskilled
> weapon rather than a weapon I have some skill in, but that's fairly
> trivial.

Two points of damage, the difference between a -1 and a -3 penalty.

> Am I right in all of this? 

Afraid not. I wish I could find a formulation of this that people
found easier to understand...

> And is this why #twoweapon combat only exercises the #twoweapon skill,
> and never the individual weapon skills? After all, given the above,
> the exercise of such skills wouldn't matter.

It'll matter a great deal for those roles that can get better than
basic in two-weapon. As to the reason why, "balance" is probably your
answer :-)

